{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"drab-goldfoil-sifaka","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# The project's authoritative config never got the Bedrock switch — only a per-developer opt-in was shipped\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nEpic 061c was tracked as having THREE permanent production switches: the repo config / local env, the\nAWS review bot, and the CI external suite. The review bot is now cut over\n[rebar:eb6e-f43a-e86b-443a] and CI selects a provider through `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE`. **The repo\nconfig half was never done**, so every local gate run on this project still resolves to direct\nAnthropic.\n\nMEASURED, in a clean worktree at `gerrit/main` with no `REBAR_LLM_*` env set:\n\n    merged [llm] file table keys: (EMPTY)\n    model_classes present: False\n      frontier -> anthropic:claude-opus-4-8\n      standard -> anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6\n      trivial  -> anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5\n\n`rebar.toml` IS this project's authoritative config — `src/rebar/config.py` discovers\n`rebar.toml` then `pyproject.toml`, and `read_reserved_section(\"llm\", root)` reads a top-level\n`[llm]` table from it. But `rebar.toml`'s only sections are `[verify]`, `[ticket]`, `[mcp]`,\n`[jira]` and `[identity]`. There is no `[llm]` table, so the built-in class defaults apply and\nthose are unqualified names that infer provider `anthropic`.\n\n**What WAS shipped, and why the tracking drifted.** [rebar:1aa2-37cf-5cdf-48b7] closed legitimately\nagainst its own criteria: its Scope allowed \"local LLM configuration only (the config file AND/OR\ndocumented env for a developer machine)\" and it chose the documented-env route —\n`docs/local-dev-env.md:142-182` tells a developer to\n`export REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE=\"/Users/joeoakhart/rebar-wt-provider-seam/.github/llm-providers/bedrock.toml\"`, explicitly labelled\n\"opt in\", with a documented revert. So 1aa2 is not a false close; the epic-level summary that read\nit as \"repo config / local env — DONE\" is what overstated it. Nobody has to opt in, and by default\nnobody does.\n\n**Why it matters rather than being cosmetic.** The epic's founding premise is that the path rebar\ndevelops against is not the path rebar's users run, and that dogfooding closes the gap. A per-developer\nopt-in that defaults OFF leaves that gap open for the local gates — which are the gates a developer\nand every coding agent hit constantly. Observed while working eb6e: a local `rebar review-plan` ran\non `anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6` minutes after the production bot was verified on Bedrock.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `rebar.toml` — add an `[llm.model_classes]` table (the file is the project's authoritative config).\n- `docs/local-dev-env.md` — the opt-in section becomes an OPT-OUT section, since the default inverts.\n- possibly a pinning test, so the ids cannot drift from `.github/llm-providers/bedrock.toml` (the same\n  two-sided pin `tests/unit/test_reviewbot_bedrock_cutover.py` applies to the compose block).\n\n## Approach — and the decision this ticket actually needs\n\nThe mechanical change is small; the DECISION is not, and should be made explicitly rather than by\ncommitting a default:\n\n* **Precedence makes it safe to try.** `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE` OUTRANKS the project file, and env\n  class slots outrank both, so CI arms that name a provider overlay (the external matrix, prompt-eval)\n  are unaffected by a project default. The offline unit lanes make no LLM calls at all.\n* **The cost is that every local gate then needs working AWS credentials.** A contributor without\n  Bedrock access, or offline, would find their local gates failing where they used to work with an\n  Anthropic key. That is a real onboarding regression and is the reason this is a decision, not a\n  chore. Mitigation is a documented one-line opt-out (`REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE=…/anthropic.toml`),\n  which is the mirror of what is documented today.\n* Alternative considered: leave the default as Anthropic and instead make the opt-in loud (a\n  `make` target, or a doctor warning). Cheaper, keeps onboarding intact, but does not close the\n  dogfooding gap — it only advertises it.\n\nDo NOT set `[llm] model` as a scalar; use `[llm.model_classes]`, or the per-pass frontier/standard\nsplit collapses (the same trap [rebar:eb58-e71d-ec90-4b00] deprecated `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` for).\n\n## Testing\n\n- assert that in a clean checkout with no `REBAR_LLM_*` env and no `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE`, the three\n  classes resolve to `bedrock:` ids — i.e. the exact measurement above, inverted. Verify RED by\n  removing the table.\n- assert the ids are byte-equal to `.github/llm-providers/bedrock.toml`, two-sided, so editing either\n  file alone fails.\n- assert the documented OPT-OUT works: with `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE` pointed at the anthropic overlay,\n  resolution returns to `anthropic:` — otherwise the escape hatch is unproven.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] the decision is recorded (project default Bedrock vs loud opt-in), with the onboarding cost\n      stated, so it is a choice rather than a drift.\n- [ ] if the default flips: `rebar.toml` carries an `[llm.model_classes]` table whose three ids are\n      byte-equal to `.github/llm-providers/bedrock.toml`, pinned two-sided by a test.\n- [ ] a clean checkout with no LLM env resolves all three classes to `bedrock:` ids — recorded as\n      observed output, since the current state was only discovered by measuring rather than reading.\n- [ ] `docs/local-dev-env.md` documents the OPT-OUT (and no longer presents Bedrock as opt-in), and\n      the opt-out is exercised once.\n- [ ] the epic-level claim that the 'repo config / local env' switch is complete is corrected wherever\n      it appears, so the next reader is not misled the way this was discovered.","id":"d2ce-36f5-fd08-4e40","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Local dev still defaults to Anthropic: rebar.toml has no [llm] table, so the repo-config half of the Bedrock switch was never done"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785806429944664001,"uuid":"72022ef1-8bb5-4e76-b438-5d35bf947b76"}